FINAL ANSWER — THE UNIFIED THEORY
─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

ROOT CAUSE: Proxmox host has ZERO IO isolation between VMs on the
shared NVMe. Any single VM generating heavy IO can saturate the
entire NVMe queue, causing host-wide IO delay affecting all other VMs.

MECHANISM:
1. Any trigger causes high IO on one VM (OOM, qemu-ga, cascade, etc.)
2. No IO throttling → VM monopolizes NVMe queue
3. NVMe latency climbs from 1-3ms to 300-1300ms
4. ALL other VMs experience IO starvation
5. K8s probes (HTTP to apiserver, exec into containers) time out
6. Pods marked unhealthy, restart
7. Restarting pods do full LIST from etcd
8. More IO load on already-saturated NVMe
9. Self-sustaining cascade
10. Requires external intervention to break

FIX (PRIMARY): Per-VM IO throttling via Proxmox Hardware → Disk →
Bandwidth tab, or qm set --scsi0 with mbps_*, iops_* parameters.

FIX (SECONDARY):
- Larger probe timeouts on constrained hardware
- Sequential VM operations (never parallel)
- Scale-down procedure for break-glass recovery
- Adequate VM memory sizing (masters 4 GB minimum recommended)

